I now have a complete picture. Here is my thorough report.

## Summary

Owner (= "member") registration is a single endpoint, `POST /api/owners`, handled by `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner(...)`. `registrationDate`, `customerCode`, and `membershipNumber` are all owner fields; the membership number's "year segment" is derived from `registrationDate`. The daily create-limit is enforced by a dedicated validator. There is **no** business-day utility in main code — only a test helper — even though an acceptance test asserts the default registration date must be a business day.

---

## 1. Controller(s) and Service(s) handling owner/member registration

**Controller (create):** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v1/OwnerRestControllerV1.java`
- `addOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` — lines 145-174. This is the create flow. It runs validators, normalizers, maps the DTO to an `Owner`, assigns `customerCode`/`namesakeCount`/household, and saves. Key excerpt (lines 147-169):
```java
public ResponseEntity<OwnerDto> addOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerFieldsDto) {
    ownerFieldsDto.setAddress(this.addressNormalizer.normalize(ownerFieldsDto.getAddress()));
    this.ownerRequestValidator.validate(ownerFieldsDto);
    this.householdDuplicateValidator.validate(ownerFieldsDto);
    this.cityOwnerLimitValidator.validate(ownerFieldsDto);
    this.dailyOwnerLimitValidator.validate();               // <-- daily create-limit
    ...
    Owner owner = ownerMapper.toOwner(ownerFieldsDto);      // maps registrationDate if supplied
    owner.setCustomerCode(
        this.customerCodeGenerator.generate(owner.getCity(), owner.getLastName(),
            this.clinicService.countOwnersInCity(owner.getCity())));
    owner.setNamesakeCount(
        (int) this.clinicService.countNamesakes(owner.getFirstName(), owner.getLastName()));
    if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(ownerFieldsDto.getSharesHousehold())) {
        this.householdRegistrar.register(owner);
    }
    this.clinicService.saveOwner(owner);                    // triggers @PrePersist default
    OwnerDto ownerDto = ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner);      // reads derived membershipNumber
    ...
}
```

**Controller (V2, read-only):** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/controller/v2/OwnerRestControllerV2.java` — only `listOwnersPage`; not involved in creation.

**Service:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/service/ClinicServiceImpl.java` (interface `ClinicService.java`)
- `saveOwner(Owner)` — lines 240-244.
- `countOwnersRegisteredOn(LocalDate)` — lines 270-274 (delegates to `ownerRepository.countByRegistrationDate`).
- `countOwnersInCity`, `countNamesakes` — lines 264-268 (used for customer code / namesake count).

**Mapper:** `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` — `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` (lines 26-28) is MapStruct-generated; it maps `registrationDate` by name from the DTO to the entity (only `id` and `pets` are ignored), so a client-supplied `registrationDate` flows through.

---

## 2. Where `registrationDate` is set

Field + defaulting live in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`:
- Field: lines 54-55 `@Column(name = "registration_date") private LocalDate registrationDate;`
- Getter/Setter: lines 101-107.
- **Server-date default via JPA lifecycle callback** — lines 176-181:
```java
@PrePersist
private void defaultRegistrationDate() {
    if (this.registrationDate == null) {
        this.registrationDate = LocalDate.now();
    }
}
```
So a **supplied** date (from `OwnerFieldsDto.registrationDate`, mapped in the controller at line 160) is kept as-is; when **null**, it defaults to `LocalDate.now()` at persist time.

Important: this default runs only on the JPA path (`@PrePersist`). The JDBC repo (`JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl`, line 167 `BeanPropertySqlParameterSource`) would not trigger `@PrePersist`, so under JDBC the default would not be applied. The Spring Data JPA repository (`SpringDataOwnerRepository`) is the one that fires the callback.

OpenAPI contract: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` lines 2047-2055 — `registrationDate` is optional on create, defaults to server current date, returned ISO `YYYY-MM-DD`.

---

## 3. Where the membership number is generated ("year segment" from registration date)

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`, `getMembershipNumber()` — lines 133-144 (this is the derived/computed field, no setter):
```java
public String getMembershipNumber() {
    if (this.customerCode == null || this.registrationDate == null) {
        return null;
    }
    return String.format("%s-M%02d", this.customerCode, this.registrationDate.getYear() % 100);
}
```
The **year segment** is `this.registrationDate.getYear() % 100` (last two digits), formatted `%02d`, e.g. `SMI-0007-M26`. Contract documented in `openapi.yml` lines 2117-2124.

Related generated pieces feeding the membership number:
- `customerCode`: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/assignment/CustomerCodeGenerator.java`, `generate(city, lastName, cityOwnerCount)` lines 38-40 → `String.format("%s-%s-%04d", ...)`.
- Membership tier (`getMembershipTier`, lines 151-155) and locality (lines 161-170) are also derived on the entity, adjacent to the membership number, if relevant.

---

## 4. Where the daily create-limit is enforced

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/validation/DailyOwnerLimitValidator.java` (whole file, 30-50):
```java
static final long MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY = 100;
...
public void validate() {
    LocalDate today = LocalDate.now();
    if (this.clinicService.countOwnersRegisteredOn(today) >= MAX_OWNERS_PER_DAY) {
        throw new DailyOwnerLimitExceededException(today);
    }
}
```
- Invoked from `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` at line 152.
- Counting chain: `ClinicServiceImpl.countOwnersRegisteredOn` (lines 270-274) → `OwnerRepository.countByRegistrationDate` (interface lines 78-84) with implementations:
  - Spring Data JPA: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/springdatajpa/SpringDataOwnerRepository.java` lines 74-75 (`@Query ... WHERE owner.registrationDate = :registrationDate`).
  - JPA: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/jpa/JpaOwnerRepositoryImpl.java` lines 158-161.
  - JDBC: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/repository/jdbc/JdbcOwnerRepositoryImpl.java` lines 263-268 (`SELECT COUNT(*) FROM owners WHERE registration_date=:registrationDate`).
- Exception: `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/error/DailyOwnerLimitExceededException.java`; handled in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/advice/ExceptionControllerAdvice.java` around line 323.

Note the limit is counted against `LocalDate.now()` (today), while the actual stored `registrationDate` could be a supplied past/future date — the validator does not count against the supplied date.

---

## 5. Existing date / business-day utility classes

There is **no** business-day (weekend/holiday roll) utility in main source. The only utility classes are:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/EntityUtils.java` — entity lookup only, no date logic.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/CallMonitoringAspect.java` — monitoring aspect, unrelated.

The only business-day logic that exists is a **test helper**: `AcceptanceBase.toBusinessDay(LocalDate)` at `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` lines 268-274, backed by a fixed `HOLIDAYS` set (lines 240-242):
```java
protected LocalDate toBusinessDay(LocalDate d) {
    while (d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SATURDAY || d.getDayOfWeek() == DayOfWeek.SUNDAY
            || HOLIDAYS.contains(d)) {
        d = d.plusDays(1);
    }
    return d;
}
```
Current production `defaultRegistrationDate()` (Owner.java lines 176-181) does **not** apply any business-day roll — it uses raw `LocalDate.now()`. If you need the default to land on a business day, that logic does not yet exist in main and would need to be added (likely in `Owner.@PrePersist` or a new utility injected into the create flow).

---

## 6. Relevant test files

- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` — `coreDefaultDateIsBusinessDay()` (lines 14-19) asserts the returned `registrationDate` is never Saturday/Sunday. This is the black-box test for the business-day default.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — shared base: create/fetch helpers (`createOwnerOk`, `fetchOwner`, `createMany` for per-day limit at lines 181-187, `fillCity`), pinned `HOLIDAYS`, and the `toBusinessDay` reference implementation. `@Transactional` rollback keeps count-based rules deterministic.

These two are the only tests referencing registration date / membership / daily-limit behavior. Note there are no dedicated unit tests for `DailyOwnerLimitValidator`, `CustomerCodeGenerator`, or `Owner.getMembershipNumber()` — coverage of those is only through the acceptance layer.

---

## Notes for implementing a change
- To make the default registration date a business day: modify `Owner.defaultRegistrationDate()` (Owner.java:176-181), or introduce a real business-day utility in `util/` (mirroring `AcceptanceBase.toBusinessDay` + `HOLIDAYS`) and apply it there. The membership number year segment (Owner.java:143) automatically follows whatever `registrationDate` ends up being.
- The daily-limit counts against `LocalDate.now()` (DailyOwnerLimitValidator.java:45), not the (possibly supplied or business-day-rolled) `registrationDate` — worth aligning if the semantics change.

